The code statements that lead to the bug are:
sg_init_table(sgl->sg, MAX_SGL_ENTS + 1);
if (sg)
    sg_chain(sg, MAX_SGL_ENTS + 1, sgl->sg);
Reason:
sg_init_table sets the SG_END flag on the last entry of the SGL (index MAX_SGL_ENTS). When a subsequent sendmsg() chains a new SGL via sg_chain(), the end marker on the previous SGL's last data entry (index MAX_SGL_ENTS - 1) is not cleared. This causes sg_next() to treat that entry as the end of the entire scatterlist, returning NULL prematurely and leading to a NULL pointer dereference in the crypto scatterwalk.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
if (sg) {
    sg_unmark_end(sg + MAX_SGL_ENTS - 1);
    sg_chain(sg, MAX_SGL_ENTS + 1, sgl->sg);
}
Reason:
By calling sg_unmark_end(sg + MAX_SGL_ENTS - 1) before sg_chain(), the end marker on the last data entry of the previous SGL is explicitly cleared. This ensures that sg_chain() sets the chain pointer correctly, and sg_next() will continue to the next SGL instead of returning NULL prematurely, thus preventing the NULL pointer dereference.

